# Frontend Development Patterns

## Architecture (TALL Stack)
- **Livewire/Volt**: Single-file components in `resources/views/livewire` for dynamic UI and business logic.
- **Tailwind CSS 4**: Utility-first styling with a custom theme defined in `resources/css/app.css`.
- **Alpine.js**: Integrated automatically by Livewire for client-side interactions.
- **Flux UI**: The primary component library for building a consistent user interface.

## Directory Structure
The `resources/views` directory is organized by feature and component type.

```
resources/views/
├── components/          # Reusable Blade components
│   ├── admin/           # Admin-specific components
│   ├── dashboard/       # Dashboard cards (stat-card, action-card)
│   ├── layouts/         # Main layout wrappers (app, auth)
│   └── settings/        # Settings page components
├── flux/                # Flux UI component overrides/customizations
│   └── icon/            # SVG icon components
├── livewire/            # Livewire Volt component files
│   ├── admin/           # Admin functionality (data, inventory, system)
│   ├── auth/            # Authentication views
│   ├── inventory-manager/# Inventory manager views
│   └── settings/        # User settings views
└── partials/            # Partial Blade views (e.g., navigation menus)
```

## Component Patterns

### Volt Single-File Components
Volt components combine PHP logic and Blade templates into a single file. They use attributes for layouts and computed properties for efficient data handling.

```php
// resources/views/livewire/admin/main/dashboard.blade.php
<?php
use Livewire\Attributes\{Layout, Computed};
use Livewire\Volt\Component;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Cache;
use App\Models\User;

new #[Layout('components.layouts.app')] class extends Component {
    #[Computed]
    public function userCount(): int {
        return Cache::remember('admin.dashboard.user_count', now()->addMinutes(5),
            fn() => User::count());
    }
}; ?>

<div>
    <x-dashboard.stat-card title="Total Users" :value="$this->userCount" />
</div>
```

### Layouts
- **App Layout**: The main application wrapper, which includes a responsive sidebar for navigation.
  ```blade
  // resources/views/components/layouts/app.blade.php
  <x-layouts.app.sidebar :title="$title ?? null">
      <flux:main>
          {{ $slot }}
      </flux:main>
  </x-layouts.app.sidebar>
  ```
- **Role-Specific Navigation**: The sidebar dynamically includes navigation partials based on the authenticated user's role.
  ```blade
  // resources/views/components/layouts/app/sidebar.blade.php
  @if (auth()->user()->adminUser)
      @include('partials.navigation.admin')
  @elseif (auth()->user()->divisionInventoryManager)
      @include('partials.navigation.inventory-manager')
  @endif
  ```

### UI Components

#### Dashboard Components
Custom Blade components are used for dashboard elements like statistics cards.

```blade
<!-- resources/views/components/dashboard/stat-card.blade.php -->
@props(['title', 'value', 'subtitle' => null])
<div {{ $attributes->merge(['class' => 'p-4 rounded-lg shadow-sm bg-stone-50/50 dark:bg-stone-900/50']) }}>
    <h4 class="font-medium text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">{{ $title }}</h4>
    <p class="text-3xl font-bold mt-1 text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">{{ $value }}</p>
    @if($subtitle)
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">{{ $subtitle }}</p>
    @endif
</div>
```

#### Flux UI Components
Flux UI is used for most UI elements, including buttons, inputs, and more.

- **Buttons**: Variants include `primary`, `ghost`, `filled`, and `danger`.
  ```blade
  <flux:button variant="primary" type="submit">{{ __('Save') }}</flux:button>
  <flux:button :href="route('dashboard')" wire:navigate variant="ghost">{{ __('Cancel') }}</flux:button>
  <flux:button variant="danger" wire:click="delete">{{ __('Delete') }}</flux:button>
  ```

- **Form Inputs**:
  ```blade
  <flux:input wire:model="name" :label="__('Name')" type="text" required />
  <flux:password wire:model="password" :label="__('Password')" required viewable />
  <flux:checkbox wire:model="remember" :label="__('Remember me')" />
  ```

- **Icons**: Icons are referenced by name from the Flux library.
  ```blade
  <x-flux::icon.users />
  <x-flux::icon.chart-bar variant="mini" />
  ```

## Styling

### Tailwind Theming
The visual theme is defined using CSS variables inside an `@theme` block in `resources/css/app.css`.

```css
/* resources/css/app.css */
@theme {
    --font-sans: 'Instrument Sans', ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
    --color-accent: var(--color-green-700);
    /* ... other variables */
}

@layer theme {
    .dark {
        --color-accent: var(--color-green-600);
    }
}
```

### Responsive Design
The UI is built mobile-first using Tailwind's responsive breakpoints. The main layout features a responsive sidebar that collapses on smaller screens.

## Form & Data Handling

### Validation
Form validation is handled within the Volt component's PHP block using `$this->validate()`.

```php
// resources/views/livewire/settings/profile.blade.php
public function updateProfileInformation(): void
{
    $user = Auth::user();

    $validated = $this->validate([
        'name' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255'],
        'email' => ['required', 'string', 'lowercase', 'email', 'max:255', Rule::unique(User::class)->ignore($user->id)],
    ]);

    $user->fill($validated)->save();
    $this->dispatch('profile-updated', name: $user->name);
}
```

### Navigation
SPA-like navigation is achieved by adding the `wire:navigate` attribute to links.

```blade
<flux:link :href="route('settings.profile')" wire:navigate>
    {{ __('Settings') }}
</flux:link>
```

## Best Practices

1.  **Volt Components**: Encapsulate all component logic within single-file Volt components.
2.  **Component Architecture**: Decompose the UI into small, reusable Blade and Livewire components.
3.  **Flux UI**: Utilize Flux components to maintain a consistent look and feel. Adhere to the defined button variants.
4.  **Validation**: Place validation logic directly within the corresponding action method in the component.
5.  **Localization**: Use Laravel's `__()` helper for all user-facing text to support localization.
6.  **Responsiveness**: Ensure all UI elements are fully responsive and support dark mode.
7.  **Navigation**: Use `wire:navigate` for seamless page transitions.
8.  **Loading States**: Use `wire:loading` to provide visual feedback during server actions.



9. **Loading States**: Use `wire:loading` for better UX during actions
10. **Form Handling**: Prefer `wire:model` binding over manual form handling


